NOTES_HTML='''<p>Michelin: the 2025 Washington, D.C. selection, announced November 18, 2025 in Philadelphia as part of the first Northeast Cities guide, holds twenty-five stars — three two-stars (minibar, Jônt, and the Inn at Little Washington, moved down from three) and twenty-two one-stars — and twenty-eight Bib Gourmands, with PhoXotic and Your Only Friend the two additions. No new stars were awarded, a first for the city. Three starred restaurants have since closed: Reverie (October 2025), Tail Up Goat (late 2025) and Cranes (February 2026).</p><p>Bars: North America's 50 Best Bars 2026 placed Silver Lyan (№55) and Allegory (№57) on its 51–100 list; Service Bar was №23 in 2025. Restaurant rankings are from Washingtonian's 100 Very Best Restaurants 2026 (Albi №1) and The Washington Post's 10 Best New Restaurants, published May 2026 by critic Elazar Sontag. James Beard marks are the 2026 nominations: Suresh Sundas of Tapori (Best Chef, Mid-Atlantic), Susan Bae of Moon Rabbit (Outstanding Pastry Chef), Chai Pani's Meherwan and Molly Irani (Outstanding Restaurateur), Maison Bar à Vins, Maxwell Park and Field & Main; semifinalists are noted where they matter.</p><p>Occasion chips for DC are editorial assignments, made by category and price, until a companion Guide by Occasion exists for the city. Booking platforms are listed as best known at time of printing; the Resy app is the source of truth for the Amex credit, which works the same way here as in New York.</p><p>Anything marked <em>verify</em> is a place whose current status couldn't be confirmed for this edition — DC lost a record number of restaurants in 2025 and the churn continued into 2026. Thirty seconds on Resy or Google before you get in a cab.</p><p>Photography via Pexels (royalty-free): six photographs, cropped and reused across the twelve sections.</p>'''
OCC_LEGEND='occasion (editorial, by category and price)'
